= Serial-position effect =

== Overview ==
The '''serial-position effect''' is systematic variation in memory or response as a function of an item's position in a sequence.

== Primacy and recency ==
Two classic components are the [[Primacy effect]], in which early items receive an advantage, and the [[Recency effect]], in which late items receive an advantage. Free-recall curves often show both.

== Mechanisms ==
Different tasks can produce serial-position effects through different combinations of rehearsal, temporal distinctiveness, retrieval context, interference, attention, and memory systems. The behavioral curve does not establish one mechanism.

== Artificial systems ==
Language models also show position-sensitive behavior, including primacy, recency, and reduced use of information in the middle of long contexts. Similar response profiles do not establish mechanistic equivalence with human memory.

== References ==
* Hermann Ebbinghaus, ''Memory'' (1885/1913).
* Bennet B. Murdock Jr., “The Serial Position Effect of Free Recall”, ''Journal of Experimental Psychology'' 64 (1962): 482–488.
* Nelson Cowan, memory and serial-position literature.

